The arguments provided to the tool are invalid: Invalid input for tool write: JSON parsing failed: Text: {"filePath": "/home/soumrnjn/Desktop/Task/SQL/sql-handbook/7-Dates-and-Strings/62-MoM-YoY-Rolling-Metrics.md", "content": "# MoM, YoY & Rolling Metrics\n\n> **What it is:** Period-over-period comparison and rolling-window metrics — the core\n> building blocks of business analytics dashboards, financial reporting, and KPI tracking.\n>\n> Cross-references: [Section 61 — Monthly & Daily Reporting], [Section 48 — LAG & LEAD],\n> [Section 50 — Moving Averages], [Section 51 — Window Frames],\n> [Section 57 — Date & Time Basics], [Section 58 — Date Arithmetic].\n\n---\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n1. [Why This Matters](#1-why-this-matters)\n2. [Core Vocabulary](#2-core-vocabulary)\n3. [Sample Tables & Data](#3-sample-tables--data)\n4. [Grain Awareness](#4-grain-awareness)\n5. [Month-over-Month (MoM)](#5-month-over-month-mom)\n6. [Year-over-Year (YoY)](#6-year-over-year-yoy)\n7. [Quarter-over-Quarter (QoQ)](#7-quarter-over-quarter-qoq)\n8. [Rolling / Trailing Window Metrics](#8-rolling--trailing-window-metrics)\n9. [Period-to-Date Metrics (MTD / QTD / YTD)](#9-period-to-date-metrics-mtd--qtd--ytd)\n10. [Growth Rate Calculations](#10-growth-rate-calculations)\n11. [Handling Missing Periods & Gap Filling](#11-handling-missing-periods--gap-filling)\n12. [Cohort-Level Rolling Metrics](#12-cohort-level-rolling-metrics)\n13. [Fiscal Period Adjustments](#13-fiscal-period-adjustments)\n14. [Internal Working](#14-internal-working)\n15. [Edge Cases & NULL Behavior](#15-edge-cases--null-behavior)\n16. [BAD vs BETTER Approaches](#16-bad-vs-better-approaches)\n17. [Common Mistakes](#17-common-mistakes)\n18. [Production Pitfalls](#18-production-pitfalls)\n19. [Performance Implications](#19-performance-implications)\n20. [Comparison Tables](#20-comparison-tables)\n21. [Best Practices](#21-best-practices)\n22. [Cross-References](#22-cross-references)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why This Matters\n\nEvery business dashboard answers the same fundamental question: **\"Is things getting better or worse?\"** Raw monthly revenue of $500,000 tells you nothing without context. Is that up from last month? Down from last year? Part of a 6-month upward trend?\n\nMoM, YoY, and rolling metrics provide that context.\n\n| Business Question | Metric Type | What It Compares |\n|---|---|---|\n| \"Are we growing compared to last month?\" | MoM | Current month vs. previous month |\n| \"How does this February compare to last February?\" | YoY | Current month vs. same month, prior year |\n| \"What's our trailing 12-month revenue?\" | Rolling (TTM) | Sum of last 12 months at each point |\n| \"What's our year-to-date revenue?\" | YTD | Cumulative from Jan 1 to today |\n| \"What's the 3-month moving average?\" | Rolling (MA) | Average of last 3 months |\n\n> **Interview trap:** Interviewers often test whether you confuse **MoM growth** (short-term, volatile) with **YoY growth** (seasonality-adjusted). Understanding when to use each is a sign of analytical maturity.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Core Vocabulary\n\n| Term | Definition | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **MoM** | Month-over-Month: compare current month to the immediately previous month | Jan revenue vs. Dec revenue |\n| **QoQ** | Quarter-over-Quarter: compare current quarter to the previous quarter | Q1 vs. Q4 |\n| **YoY** | Year-over-Year: compare current month/quarter to the same period one year ago | Jan 2025 vs. Jan 2024 |\n| **WoW** | Week-over-Week: compare current week to the previous week | Week 3 vs. Week 2 |\n| **DoD** | Day-over-Day: compare today to yesterday | Tue vs. Mon |\n| **MTD** | Month-to-Date: cumulative metric from the 1st of the current month to today | Jan 1 – Jan 15 revenue |\n| **QTD** | Quarter-to-Date: cumulative from the 1st of the current quarter to today | Jan 1 – Feb 15 revenue |\n| **YTD** | Year-to-Date: cumulative from Jan 1 to today | Jan 1 – Jun 30 revenue |\n| **TTM** / **LTM** | Trailing Twelve Months / Last Twelve Months: rolling 12-month sum | Sum of Jul 2024 – Jun 2025 |\n| **Rolling Avg** | Moving average over a trailing window | Average of last 3 months |\n| **Growth Rate** | Percentage change: `(current - previous) / previous × 100` | (500K - 450K) / 450K = 11.1% |\n\n```mermaid\ngraph LR\n    subgraph \"Period-over-Period\"\n        A[Current Period] -->|Compare to| B[Previous Period]\n        A -->|Compare to| C[Same Period, Prior Year]\n    end\n    subgraph \"Rolling Windows\"\n        D[Current Point] -->|Look back N periods| E[Trailing Window]\n        E -->|Aggregate| F[Sum / Avg / Count]\n    end\n    subgraph \"Period-to-Date\"\n        G[Period Start] -->|Accumulate to| H[Current Point]\n    end\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Sample Tables & Data\n\n### 3.1 Monthly Revenue Table (Grain: one row per month)\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE monthly_revenue (\n    month_date   DATE PRIMARY KEY,      -- first day of each month\n    revenue      DECIMAL(12,2),\n    new_customers INT,\n    orders_count  INT\n);\n\nINSERT INTO monthly_revenue VALUES\n    ('2024-01-01', 120000.00, 320, 1800),\n    ('2024-02-01', 135000.00, 380, 2050),\n    ('2024-03-01', 142000.00, 410, 2200),\n    ('2024-04-01', 128000.00, 290, 1900),\n    ('2024-05-01', 155000.00, 450, 2400),\n    ('2024-06-01', 168000.00, 480, 2600),\n    ('2024-07-01', 145000.00, 340, 2100),\n    ('2024-08-01', 162000.00, 420, 2350),\n    ('2024-09-01', 178000.00, 510, 2700),\n    ('2024-10-01', 190000.00, 540, 2900),\n    ('2024-11-01', 210000.00, 620, 3200),\n    ('2024-12-01', 245000.00, 710, 3800),\n    ('2025-01-01', 150000.00, 400, 2300),\n    ('2025-02-01', 165000.00, 460, 2500),\n    ('2025-03-01', 175000.00, 500, 2650),\n    ('2025-04-01', 160000.00, 440, 2400),\n    ('2025-05-01', 185000.00, 530, 2800),\n    ('2025-06-01', 200000.00, 580, 3000);\n```\n\n**Grain:** One row = one calendar month. All metrics in that row are totals for that month.\n\n### 3.2 Daily Orders Table (Grain: one row per order)\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE orders (\n    order_id    INT PRIMARY KEY,\n    customer_id INT,\n    order_date  DATE,\n    amount      DECIMAL(10,2)\n);\n\n-- Grain: one row = one order placed on a specific date\n```\n\n**Production pitfall:** Always know the grain before writing any comparison query. Comparing a daily-grain column against a monthly-grain column produces meaningless results.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Grain Awareness\n\nBefore writing any period-over-period comparison, ask:\n\n1. **What does one output row represent?**\n2. **What is the grain of the source data?**\n3. **Are both periods being compared at the same grain?**\n\n| Common Error | What Goes Wrong |\n|---|---|\n| Comparing daily sum to monthly sum | Numbers are orders of magnitude apart |\n| Comparing rolling 30-day sum to calendar month sum | Different time windows produce different totals |\n| Comparing MoM for a metric that is cumulative (like YTD) | Always shows a positive jump each month |\n| Comparing daily MoM when data has gaps | Gap-free months show zero growth |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Month-over-Month (MoM)\n\n### 5.1 Using LAG()\n\n**BETTER APPROACH:**\n\n```sql\nWITH monthly AS (\n    SELECT\n        month_date,\n        revenue,\n        LAG(revenue, 1) OVER (ORDER BY month_date) AS prev_month_revenue\n    FROM monthly_revenue\n)\nSELECT\n    month_date,\n    revenue,\n    prev_month_revenue,\n    revenue - prev_month_revenue                        AS abs_change,\n    ROUND(\n        (revenue - prev_month_revenue)\n        / NULLIF(prev_month_revenue, 0) * 100,\n        2\n    )                                                   AS mom_pct\nFROM monthly\nORDER BY month_date;\n```\n\n**Expected output (excerpt):**\n\n| month_date | revenue | prev_month_revenue | abs_change | mom_pct |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2024-01-01 | 120000.00 | NULL | NULL | NULL |\n| 2024-02-01 | 135000.00 | 120000.00 | 15000.00 | 12.50 |\n| 2024-03-01 | 142000.00 | 135000.00 | 7000.00 | 5.19 |\n| 2024-04-01 | 128000.00 | 142000.00 | -14000.00 | -9.86 |\n| 2024-05-01 | 155000.00 | 128000.00 | 27000.00 | 21.09 |\n| 2024-06-01 | 168000.00 | 155000.00 | 13000.00 | 8.39 |\n\n### 5.2 Using Self-Join\n\n**BETTER APPROACH** (works on all databases, no window functions needed):\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n    curr.month_date,\n    curr.revenue,\n    prev.revenue                                           AS prev_month_revenue,\n    curr.revenue - prev.revenue                           AS abs_change,\n    ROUND(\n        (curr.revenue - prev.revenue)\n        / NULLIF(prev.revenue, 0) * 100,\n        2\n    )                                                      AS mom_pct\nFROM monthly_revenue curr\nLEFT JOIN monthly_revenue prev\n    ON prev.month_date = curr.month_date - INTERVAL '1 month'\nORDER BY curr.month_date;\n```\n\n### 5.3 MoM on Raw Order-Level Data\n\nWhen you need MoM from an unaggregated `orders` table:\n\n```sql\nWITH monthly AS (\n    SELECT\n        DATE_TRUNC('month', order_date)::DATE AS month_date,\n        SUM(amount)                          AS revenue\n    FROM orders\n    WHERE order_date >= '2024-01-01'\n      AND order_date <  '2025-07-01'\n    GROUP BY DATE_TRUNC('month', order_date)\n)\nSELECT\n    month_date,\n    revenue,\n    LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month_date) AS prev_month_revenue,\n    ROUND(\n        (revenue - LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month_date))\n        / NULLIF(LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month_date), 0) * 100,\n        2\n    ) AS mom_pct\nFROM monthly\nORDER BY month_date;\n```\n\n**Production pitfall:** Always aggregate to the monthly grain **before** applying `LAG()`. Applying `LAG()` to individual order rows is meaningless — you'd be comparing one order to the previous order, not one month to the previous month.\n\n### 5.4 MoM at Different Grains\n\nThe same `LAG()` pattern works for DoD, WoW, MoM — just change the truncation:\n\n| Grain | Truncation | LAG Offset |\n|---|---|---|\n| Day-over-Day | No truncation needed | `LAG(x, 1)` |\n| Week-over-Week | `DATE_TRUNC('week', col)` | `LAG(x, 1)` |\n| Month-over-Month | `DATE_TRUNC('month', col)` | `LAG(x, 1)` |\n| Quarter-over-Quarter | `DATE_TRUNC('quarter', col)` | `LAG(x, 1)` |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Year-over-Year (YoY)\n\n### 6.1 Using LAG with Offset 12\n\n```sql\nWITH monthly AS (\n    SELECT\n        month_date,\n        revenue,\n        LAG(revenue, 12) OVER (ORDER BY month_date) AS prev_year_revenue\n    FROM monthly_revenue\n)\nSELECT\n    month_date,\n    revenue,\n    prev_year_revenue,\n    revenue - prev_year_revenue                       AS abs_change,\n    ROUND(\n        (revenue - prev_year_revenue)\n        / NULLIF(prev_year_revenue, 0) * 100,\n        2\n    )                                                  AS yoy_pct\nFROM monthly\nORDER BY month_date;\n```\n\n**Expected output (excerpt):**\n\n| month_date | revenue | prev_year_revenue | abs_change | yoy_pct |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2024-01-01 | 120000.00 | NULL | NULL | NULL |\n| 2024-12-01 | 245000.00 | NULL | NULL | NULL |\n| 2025-01-01 | 150000.00 | 120000.00 | 30000.00 | 25.00 |\n| 2025-02-01 | 165000.00 | 135000.00 | 30000.00 | 22.22 |\n| 2025-03-01 | 175000.00 | 142000.00 | 33000.00 | 23.24 |\n\n### 6.2 Using Self-Join (More Explicit)\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n    curr.month_date,\n    curr.revenue,\n    prev.revenue                                         AS prev_year_revenue,\n    ROUND(\n        (curr.revenue - prev.revenue)\n        / NULLIF(prev.revenue, 0) * 100,\n        2\n    )                                                    AS yoy_pct\nFROM monthly_revenue curr\nLEFT JOIN monthly_revenue prev\n    ON prev.month_date = curr.month_date - INTERVAL '1 year'\nORDER BY curr.month_date;\n```\n\n> **PostgreSQL:** `curr.month_date - INTERVAL '1 year'` works directly.\n>\n> **MySQL:** Use `DATE_SUB(curr.month_date, INTERVAL 1 YEAR)`.\n>\n> **SQL Server:** Use `DATEADD(YEAR, -1, curr.month_date)`.\n>\n> **Oracle:** Use `ADD_MONTHS(curr.month_date, -12)`.\n\n### 6.3 Using Self-Join with Year Extraction\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n    curr.month_date,\n    curr.revenue,\n    prev.revenue                                         AS prev_year_revenue,\n    ROUND(\n        (curr.revenue - prev.revenue)\n        / NULLIF(prev.revenue, 0) * 100,\n        2\n    )                                                    AS yoy_pct\nFROM monthly_revenue curr\nLEFT JOIN monthly_revenue prev\n    ON EXTRACT(MONTH FROM prev.month_date) = EXTRACT(MONTH FROM curr.month_date)\n   AND EXTRACT(YEAR  FROM prev.month_date) = EXTRACT(YEAR  FROM curr.year_date) - 1\nORDER BY curr.month_date;\n```\n\nThis is semantically identical to the `INTERVAL '1 year'` approach but can be useful when the period column is not a simple `DATE` (e.g., composite keys).\n\n### 6.4 YoY Is Preferred Over MoM for Seasonal Data\n\nMoM is misleading for seasonal businesses:\n\n| Month | Revenue | MoM | YoY |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Nov 2024 | 210000 | +10.5% | — |\n| Dec 2024 | 245000 | +16.7% | — |\n| Jan 2025 | 150000 | -38.8% ← alarming! | +25.0% ← healthy growth |\n\nThe Jan drop from Dec is expected (holiday season). MoM makes it look catastrophic. YoY shows the real trend: Jan 2025 is 25% above Jan 2024.\n\n> **Interview trap:** When someone asks \"is the business growing?\" — always check YoY for seasonal products, MoM for non-seasonal or short-cycle metrics.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Quarter-over-Quarter (QoQ)\n\n### 7.1 Truncating to Quarter\n\n```sql\nWITH quarterly AS (\n    SELECT\n        DATE_TRUNC('quarter', order_date)::DATE AS quarter_start,\n        SUM(amount)                            AS revenue\n    FROM orders\n    WHERE order_date >= '2024-01-01'\n      AND order_date <  '2026-01-01'\n    GROUP BY DATE_TRUNC('quarter', order_date)\n)\nSELECT\n    quarter_start,\n    revenue,\n    LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY quarter_start) AS prev_qtr_revenue,\n    ROUND(\n        (revenue - LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY quarter_start))\n        / NULLIF(LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY quarter_start), 0) * 100,\n        2\n    ) AS qoq_pct\nFROM quarterly\nORDER BY quarter_start;\n```\n\n> **PostgreSQL:** `DATE_TRUNC('quarter', col)` returns the first day of the quarter.\n>\n> **MySQL:** `MAKEDATE(YEAR(col), 1) + INTERVAL (QUARTER(col)*3 - 3) MONTH` or use a CASE expression.\n>\n> **SQL Server:** `DATEFROMPARTS(YEAR(col), (DATEPART(QUARTER, col) - 1) * 3 + 1, 1)`.\n>\n> **Oracle:** `TRUNC(col, 'Q')`.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Rolling / Trailing Window Metrics\n\n### 8.1 Rolling Sum (Trailing 12 Months / TTM)\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n    month_date,\n    revenue,\n    SUM(revenue) OVER (\n        ORDER BY month_date\n        ROWS BETWEEN 11 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW\n    ) AS trailing_12m_revenue\nFROM monthly_revenue\nORDER BY month_date;\n```\n\n**Expected output (excerpt):**\n\n| month_date | revenue | trailing_12m_revenue |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2024-01-01 | 120000.00 | 120000.00 |\n| 2024-02-01 | 135000.00 | 255000.00 |\n| 2024-03-01 | 142000.00 | 397000.00 |\n| 2024-12-01 | 245000.00 | 1968000.00 |\n| 2025-01-01 | 150000.00 | 1998000.00 ← includes Feb 2024 – Jan 2025 |\n\n> **Common misconception:** \"Trailing 12 months\" using `ROWS BETWEEN 11 PRECEDING` only works if every month has a row. If a month is missing, the window silently includes older data. See [Section 11 — Handling Missing Periods].\n\n### 8.2 Rolling Average (3-Month Moving Average)\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n    month_date,\n    revenue,\n    ROUND(AVG(revenue) OVER (\n        ORDER BY month_date\n        ROWS BETWEEN 2 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW\n    ), 2) AS rolling_3m_avg\nFROM monthly_revenue\nORDER BY month_date;\n```\n\n| month_date | revenue | rolling_3m_avg |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2024-01-01 | 120000.00 | 120000.00 ← only 1 month available |\n| 2024-02-01 | 135000.00 | 127500.00 ← avg of 2 months |\n| 2024-03-01 | 142000.00 | 132333.33 ← avg of 3 months |\n| 2024-04-01 | 128000.00 | 135000.00 |\n\n**Warm-up period caveat:** The first few rows of a rolling window average fewer periods than intended. See [Section 16 — BAD vs BETTER] for how to handle this.\n\n### 8.3 Rolling Count (Distinct Customers in Last 3 Months)\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n    month_date,\n    new_customers,\n    SUM(new_customers) OVER (\n        ORDER BY month_date\n        ROWS BETWEEN 2 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW\n    ) AS rolling_3m_new_customers\nFROM monthly_revenue\nORDER BY month_date;\n```\n\n> **Production pitfall:** Rolling `SUM` of monthly aggregates works because the monthly table is already deduplicated. If you try to compute `COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id)` in a rolling window, you **cannot** simply sum distinct counts from sub-periods — customers overlap. Use a raw event table with a windowed subquery or approximate counting (HyperLogLog). See [Section 12 — Cohort-Level Rolling Metrics].\n\n### 8.4 Rolling MAX / MIN\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n    month_date,\n    revenue,\n    MAX(revenue) OVER (\n        ORDER BY month_date\n        ROWS BETWEEN 2 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW\n    ) AS rolling_3m_max,\n    MIN(revenue) OVER (\n        ORDER BY month_date\n        ROWS BETWEEN 2 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW\n    ) AS rolling_3m_min\nFROM monthly_revenue\nORDER BY month_date;\n```\n\n### 8.5 Rolling Growth Rate\n\n```sql\nWITH monthly AS (\n    SELECT\n        month_date,\n        revenue,\n        SUM(revenue) OVER (\n            ORDER BY month_date\n            ROWS BETWEEN 11 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW\n        ) AS trailing_12m_revenue\n    FROM monthly_revenue\n)\nSELECT\n    month_date,\n    trailing_12m_revenue,\n    LAG(trailing_12m_revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month_date) AS prev_ttm,\n    ROUND(\n        (trailing_12m_revenue - LAG(trailing_12m_revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month_date))\n        / NULLIF(LAG(trailing_12m_revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month_date), 0) * 100,\n        2\n    ) AS ttm_growth_pct\nFROM monthly\nORDER BY month_date;\n```\n\n---\n\n## 9. Period-to-Date Metrics (MTD / QTD / YTD)\n\n### 9.1 Month-to-Date (MTD)\n\nFrom a daily orders table:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n    order_date,\n    amount,\n    SUM(amount) OVER (\n        PARTITION BY DATE_TRUNC('month', order_date)\n        ORDER BY order_date\n        ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW\n    ) AS mtd_revenue\nFROM orders\nWHERE order_date >= '2025-01-01'\n  AND order_date <  '2025-02-01'\nORDER BY order_date;\n```\n\n### 9.2 Year-to-Date (YTD)\n\nFrom the monthly table:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n    month_date,\n    revenue,\n    SUM(revenue) OVER (\n        PARTITION BY EXTRACT(YEAR FROM month_date)\n        ORDER BY month_date\n        ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW\n    ) AS ytd_revenue\nFROM monthly_revenue\nWHERE month_date >= '2025-01-01'\nORDER BY month_date;\n```\n\n**Expected output:**\n\n| month_date | revenue | ytd_revenue |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2025-01-01 | 150000.00 | 150000.00 |\n| 2025-02-01 | 165000.00 | 315000.00 |\n| 2025-03-01 | 175000.00 | 490000.00 |\n| 2025-04-01 | 160000.00 | 650000.00 |\n| 2025-05-01 | 185000.00 | 835000.00 |\n| 2025-06-01 | 200000.00 | 1035000.00 |\n\n### 9.3 Quarter-to-Date (QTD)\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n    month_date,\n    revenue,\n    SUM(revenue) OVER (\n        PARTITION BY DATE_TRUNC('quarter', month_date)\n        ORDER BY month_date\n        ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW\n    ) AS qtd_revenue\nFROM monthly_revenue\nWHERE month_date >= '2025-01-01'\nORDER BY month_date;\n```\n\n### 9.4 YTD Comparison to Prior Year\n\n```sql\nWITH monthly AS (\n    SELECT\n        month_date,\n        revenue,\n        EXTRACT(YEAR  FROM month_date)  AS yr,\n        EXTRACT(MONTH FROM month_date)  AS mo,\n        SUM(revenue) OVER (\n            PARTITION BY EXTRACT(YEAR FROM month_date)\n            ORDER BY month_date\n            ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW\n        ) AS ytd_revenue\n    FROM monthly_revenue\n    WHERE month_date >= '2024-01-01'\n)\nSELECT\n    curr.month_date,\n    curr.ytd_revenue AS ytd_2025,\n    prev.ytd_revenue AS ytd_2024,\n    ROUND(\n        (curr.ytd_revenue - prev.ytd_revenue)\n        / NULLIF(prev.ytd_revenue, 0) * 100,\n        2\n    ) AS ytd_yoy_pct\nFROM monthly curr\nLEFT JOIN monthly prev\n    ON prev.yr = curr.yr - 1\n   AND prev.mo = curr.mo\nWHERE curr.yr = 2025\nORDER BY curr.month_date;\n```\n\n---\n\n## 10. Growth Rate Calculations\n\n### 10.1 Basic Growth Rate Formula\n\n```\ngrowth_pct = (current - previous) / previous × 100\n```\n\n**Critical requirement:** Use `NULLIF(previous, 0)` to avoid division by zero.\n\n```sql\nROUND(\n    (current_value - previous_value)\n    / NULLIF(previous_value, 0) * 100,\n    2\n) AS growth_pct\n```\n\n### 10.2 When Previous Value Is Zero\n\n| Previous | Current | Growth Rate | Problem |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 100 | 150 | 50.0% | Normal |\n| 100 | 50 | -50.0% | Normal decline |\n| 0 | 500 | NULL (div by zero) | Undefined — first sale ever |\n| 0 | 0 | NULL | No change, no growth |\n| NULL | 500 | NULL | Missing previous period |\n\n> **Interview trap:** \"What is the growth rate from 0 to 500?\" Answer: it is undefined. Many candidates write `COALESCE` to make it 0, but that is semantically incorrect — growth from zero is mathematically infinite. Report as `NULL` or `N/A` and annotate.\n\n### 10.3 Compound Growth (MoM Compounding)\n\nMoM growth compounds: a 10% MoM increase each month for 12 months yields approximately `1.10^12 ≈ 3.14×` annual growth.\n\n```sql\nWITH monthly AS (\n    SELECT\n        month_date,\n        revenue,\n        ROUND(\n            (revenue - LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month_date))\n            / NULLIF(LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month_date), 0) * 100,\n            2\n        ) AS mom_pct\n    FROM monthly_revenue\n)\nSELECT\n    month_date,\n    mom_pct,\n    EXP(SUM(LN(1 + mom_pct / 100)) OVER (\n        ORDER BY month_date\n        ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW\n    )) - 1 AS cumulative_growth_factor\nFROM monthly\nWHERE mom_pct IS NOT NULL\nORDER BY month_date;\n```\n\n> **Edge case:** If any MoM value is ≤ -100%, `LN` receives zero or negative, producing `NULL` or an error. Clamp or exclude those rows.\n\n### 10.4 Average Growth Rate (CAGR)\n\n```\nCAGR = (ending_value / beginning_value)^(1 / n_periods) - 1\n```\n\n```sql\nWITH bounds AS (\n    SELECT\n        MIN(month_date) AS first_month,\n        MAX(month_date) AS last_month,\n        (SELECT revenue FROM monthly_revenue\n         WHERE month_date = MIN(month_date)) AS first_revenue,\n        (SELECT revenue FROM monthly_revenue\n         WHERE month_date = MAX(month_date)) AS last_revenue,\n        (MAX(month_date) - MIN(month_date)) / 30.0 AS months_elapsed\n    FROM monthly_revenue\n)\nSELECT\n    ROUND(\n        (POWER(last_revenue / first_revenue, 1 / (months_elapsed / 12.0)) - 1) * 100,\n        2\n    ) AS cagr_pct\nFROM bounds;\n```\n\n> **Common misconception:** CAGR assumes smooth compound growth. It hides volatility. A business with wild swings and a business with steady growth can have the same CAGR.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Handling Missing Periods & Gap Filling\n\n### 11.1 The Problem\n\nIf a month has zero orders, it is **absent** from the `monthly_revenue` table. This causes:\n\n- `LAG()` to skip a row, comparing Feb to Dec instead of Feb to Jan\n- Rolling windows to silently extend further back in time\n- Charts to show misleading gaps or jumps\n\n### 11.2 Generating a Complete Month Series\n\n**PostgreSQL:**\n\n```sql\nSELECT gs.month_date\nFROM generate_series(\n    '2024-01-01'::date,\n    '2025-06-01'::date,\n    '1 month'::interval\n) AS gs(month_date);\n```\n\n**MySQL:**\n\n```sql\nWITH RECURSIVE months AS (\n    SELECT DATE('2024-01-01') AS month_date\n    UNION ALL\n    SELECT DATE_ADD(month_date, INTERVAL 1 MONTH)\n    FROM months\n    WHERE month_date < '2025-06-01'\n)\nSELECT month_date FROM months;\n```\n\n**SQL Server:**\n\n```sql\nWITH months AS (\n    SELECT CAST('2024-01-01' AS DATE) AS month_date\n    UNION ALL\n    SELECT DATEADD(MONTH, 1, month_date)\n    FROM months\n    WHERE month_date .
