= data pages.** There is no "pointer to the heap." The data lives in the B-tree itself.
- **Sorted by cluster key.** Consecutive key values are usually on the same or adjacent leaf pages, making range scans sequential.
- **At most one.** A table has only one physical order — you cannot have two separate B-trees both claiming the rows.
- **Wide leaf pages.** Because the leaf holds full rows (potentially many columns, variable-length data), each page holds fewer key values than a "pure" index would. The tree is shallower than it looks.

