 | **Hash Join** | Build a hash table on one input, then probe it with every row of the other. | `Hash Join` (with a `Hash` build child) |
 | **Merge Join** | Walk two *already-sorted* inputs side by side and pair keys as they pass. | `Merge Join` |
 
-A fourth family — **semi-joins and anti-joins** — is not a separate mechanism; it is a specialized use of the three algorithms \u201cfor `EXISTS`/`IN`/`NOT EXISTS`/`NOT IN`" (Section 80.6).
+A fourth family — **semi-joins and anti-joins** — is not a separate mechanism; it is a specialized use of the three algorithms "for `EXISTS`/`IN`/`NOT EXISTS`/`NOT IN`" (Section 80.6).
 
 > Common misconception: "JOIN is a single thing and it's always implemented one way."
 >
 > No. `JOIN` is a *logical* operation. Nested loop, hash, and merge are the three main *physical* implementations, and each can win depending on data size, indexes, statistics, and memory.

